 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the early 2000s as a means to leverage the fixed-function pipeline of graphics hardware for volume rendering. Key developments include the use of 3D textures and proxy geometry for view-aligned slicing [S2, unofficial][S6]. The approach was further refined to handle shadows and translucency using two-pass techniques [S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in medical imaging and scientific visualization where interactive frame rates are required on standard graphics hardware.
+The technique was popularized by Krüger and Westermann in 2003 as an "Acceleration Technique for GPU-Based Volume Rendering" [S2, unofficial]. It leverages the graphics hardware's ability to rasterize textured polygons, effectively treating volume slices as proxy geometry. This approach allows for interactive frame rates by offloading the integration work to the GPU's fixed-function pipeline (alpha blending) and texture units, rather than using programmable shaders for ray marching [S2, unofficial].
 
